index c31f0b0..ef7d324 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -7,6 +7,7 @@ The format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/),
 ## [Unreleased]
 
 ### Added
+- Milestones fill the job slots (#119): every `depends_on` edge of `milestone_create` / `milestone_set` needs a one-line reason in the new `depends_why` (dependency key -> the files or modules the two tasks share, or the output one consumes); an edge without a reason is dropped and listed in the result's `depends_dropped`, a reason for a key outside `depends_on` is refused, and the tree shows each task's `depends_why` (stored in a new `milestone_tasks.depends_why` column; edges stored before keep working without one). `max_parallel` stays unset by default, so a milestone's independent tasks start up to `max_running`. Idle-slot nudge: when slots are free and tasks of open milestones are ready, the daemon types one line, `N slots free, ready: T8, T9 (milestone 1)`, once per distinct ready list. Not done: deriving edges from the paths a task will touch.
 - The job-done line flags a fast empty coder job (#132): kind fix or general that ended `succeeded` or `cancelled` within three minutes with a repository and not one commit over its base gets `· suspicious: no commits in <N>m` — such a run almost always hit a brief or sandbox mismatch instead of finishing real work. The zero-commits finding is the merge check's own (the head still at the base commit, cached like a merge note; no extra git call), so a job without a worktree is never flagged on a guess; review and research jobs and jobs without a repository are never flagged. The finding is always the latest check's own: each merge check clears it before it runs and records it again only when it sees the head at the base itself (and records "has commits" as soon as its rev-list confirms them), and a requeue (resume) drops it, so a retry whose check fails — a timeout, a missing branch, a worktree that no longer verifies — never flags a job that committed meanwhile. Only the delivered job-done line carries the note: `job_result`'s `done_line` never does — the MCP server runs its own manager, which never ran the merge check, so it has no finding to reuse.
 - OpenRouter models as job runners (#136): `model: "openrouter:<slug>"` runs a claude job on a model of config `providers.openrouter.models` through a per-job loopback proxy in the wrapper, which holds the key (read from `key_file` at start, never in config.json, claude's environment or any job file), injects the `provider` routing object (routing policy: `sort` price by default, `min_quantization` fp8, `data_collection` deny, `zdr`, `min_uptime_pct` 95, `ignore`, `order`; per model `model_routing`) and enforces the job's budget from OpenRouter's reported usage. Billing `api`; `daily_cap_usd` refuses new jobs past the cap; refused for kind review, codex and `deny_repos`; no resume. `agent-jobs openrouter endpoints <slug>` ranks a model's data-center endpoints by the policy. `job_list` / `job_status` / the HTTP API show `provider`; `job_stats --by provider`. With `providers.openrouter` set, every job's processes are kept from reading the key file's directory by Linux Landlock (the key directory must be outside `$HOME`, the temp dirs and the state dir; codex jobs on codex's native bwrap sandbox, which cannot start under any Landlock ruleset, run their codex process outside the key sandbox with a warning in stderr.log and `system_doctor` under `providers.openrouter.codex_policy` `allow_unprotected`, the default, or are refused under `refuse`); the proxy forwards only `POST /v1/messages` and `count_tokens`, reserves each request's worst case against the budget, and records a request without its final usage at its reservation with the cost unknown.
 - OpenRouter cost journal: each attempt of an OpenRouter job appends one JSON line to `<state>/openrouter-costs.jsonl` (mode 600, append-only; written on success, failure, budget refusal and on the daemon's SIGTERM) with its tokens, the proxy's cost and OpenRouter's billed record of its generations (`GET /api/v1/generation`, read by the wrapper with the key, which never reaches the line); `agent-jobs openrouter costs --agent A [--days N] [--json] [--reconcile]` lists the runs with both costs (a mismatch over 10% flagged), totals per day, per model and over 30 days, the key's usage and limit (`GET /api/v1/key`), and fills missing OpenRouter records by appending reconcile lines. The proxy removes the key and anything `sk-or-` shaped from upstream error bodies (non-200, forwarded with their own Content-Length) and from a stream's error events before claude sees them.
