 **Task status** is derived from the task's jobs, never set by hand, decided in this order: `dropped`; `running` (a job is queued, running or stopped); `review` (the latest job succeeded and has no outcome yet); `done` (the latest job's outcome is `accepted`, `extended` or `reworked`: its work was used); `failed` (the latest job failed, timed out or was cancelled, or its outcome is `discarded`); else `blocked` when a dependency that is not dropped is not `done`, else `ready` (no job yet, or the latest one's outcome is `rerun`). `blocked` replaces only `ready`: a task whose own jobs say `running`, `review`, `done` or `failed` shows that. So a task is done when you record `job_outcome(accepted)` for its job; that unblocks the tasks that depend on it.
 
+**Parallelism.** `max_parallel` is unset by default: a milestone has no cap of its own, so the independent tasks fill every free job slot (config `max_running`). Set it only to hold a milestone back. When job slots stand empty while tasks of an open milestone are ready, the daemon types one line to the orchestrator session (`notify_tmux_target`; only logged without it): `3 slots free, ready: T8, T9 (milestone 1)`. The same ready list is announced once. Tasks that share a money path or the same files keep their edge and run one after another; every job has its own worktree.
+
 **Running tasks.** `job_start` takes `milestone` (id) and `task` (key), both or neither. It is refused when the milestone is not open, the task is unknown or `dropped`, a dependency of the task is not done (the error names the dependencies it waits for; this holds for a second job on a running task too), or `repo` is not the milestone's repository. When the milestone has a `branch` that exists in the repository and `base` is not given, the job starts from that branch. Tasks without dependencies between them run in parallel, within `max_running`; `max_parallel` caps the running jobs of one milestone (1 = one after another: the daemon leaves the next job queued until a slot of the milestone is free). The milestone may change while a job waits queued, so the daemon checks it again at launch: a job whose task got a dependency that is not done stays queued until it is; a job whose task was dropped or whose milestone was closed is cancelled (`cancelled before start: task T3 of milestone 4 was dropped`). The check is taken again inside the claim's database transaction, so an edit, a job or an outcome committed between the check and the start is never missed. A job that waits says why: `waiting` in `job_status` and `job_list` (`waiting for T1 (failed)`, or a free slot under `max_parallel`), and on its task in `milestone_get`. It is never cancelled for waiting: a done dependency that got a new job which failed holds it until the dependency is done again or dropped. `job_resume` of a job whose task is dropped or whose milestone is closed is refused; one of a blocked task is queued and waits the same way. The daemon does not start ready tasks by itself.
 
 **Skills** (G01: planned once per milestone, not per job). Each task may carry `skills`, a list of edits to its jobs' default skills in `job_start`'s syntax (`name` or `+name` adds, `-name` removes, applied in order; blanks dropped; at most 20), chosen when the milestone is created or edited, so the skill choice for a whole stage is made once, in `milestone_create` (and changed with `milestone_set`). Names are checked against the skill catalog then (an unknown or deprecated name refuses the call, with close names; the per-job budget depends on the job's kind and is checked at `job_start`). A `job_start` on the task without `skills` gets the task's list as its edits; `skills` of its own (also `[]`, the plain defaults) replace the plan for that job, and a brief line `skills: none` gives it none. When the catalog no longer accepts a planned name, `job_start` on the task is refused and the error says the names were the task's plan. Skills stay optional: a task without a list has `"skills": []` and its jobs get their defaults; there is no approval step. The tree shows the plan on each task and, per job, `skills` (selected at `job_start`) and `skills_delivered` (linked into its work dir for its latest attempt); `agent-jobs milestone <id>` prints them in the `SKILLS` and `JOBS` columns. The optional `agent-jobs hook skills-picker` asks for the plan at `milestone_create` instead of at every `job_start` (see *Skills picker* under the hooks).
